1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:05,510
And our mission today is really to demystify what's called a national enterprise architecture.

2
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:10,480
Right. And, you know, once we define what a national enterprise architecture actually is- Mm.

3
00:00:10,630 --> 00:00:13,850
Uh, and we'll just say EA for short going forward,

4
00:00:14,470 --> 00:00:16,890
you realize it's essentially the ultimate shortcut.

5
00:00:17,210 --> 00:00:22,830
Yeah, a shortcut for, you know, those of you running these systems in your ministry to take control of your digital ecosystem

6
00:00:23,470 --> 00:00:24,550
rather than, um,

7
00:00:25,270 --> 00:00:27,610
rather than just being led by outside vendors or donors.

8
00:00:27,670 --> 00:00:29,570
Exactly. It's about taking the reins back.

9
00:00:29,970 --> 00:00:39,250
But I mean, when I first hear the term EA, my immediate assumption is that we're talking about, like, procuring another massively expensive software package or some big vendor tool.

10
00:00:39,290 --> 00:00:43,290
Emphatically no. That is, uh, probably the biggest misconception out there.

11
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:46,670
An EA is not a piece of software, and it's not a vendor product.

12
00:00:46,730 --> 00:00:49,990
At its core, it's really just a set of documents and diagrams.

13
00:00:50,110 --> 00:00:57,420
Just documents. Just documents. And together, they form what the briefing calls the agreed picture of how your government works.

14
00:00:57,890 --> 00:01:02,770
So mapping out the services you deliver, the data you hold, and the infrastructure running underneath it all.

15
00:01:02,830 --> 00:01:12,070
Okay, but, um, wait. If this is literally just a set of documents and diagrams, how does paper actually stop a massive tech

16
00:01:12,090 --> 00:01:17,710
vendor from bulldozing a ministry into buying, you know, unnecessary software?

17
00:01:17,750 --> 00:01:19,270
Like, paper doesn't enforce rules.

18
00:01:20,010 --> 00:01:23,980
Well, paper itself doesn't, sure, but the consensus it forces absolutely does.

19
00:01:24,070 --> 00:01:24,610
Oh, I see.

20
00:01:24,650 --> 00:01:33,590
Yeah. Without this exact shared picture, every important conversation just breaks down because you can't lead a negotiation about your digital future or even tell a vendor no

21
00:01:34,230 --> 00:01:37,950
if your own internal departments can't even agree on what your present system looks like.

22
00:01:37,990 --> 00:01:41,610
That makes total sense. So this agreed picture becomes your objective baseline.

23
00:01:41,830 --> 00:01:47,010
Exactly. Okay, so if we think of this like a master blueprint that entirely different stakeholders have to read.

24
00:01:47,050 --> 00:01:49,690
Mm. Um, who are the actual users of this picture?

25
00:01:49,710 --> 00:01:51,970
Well, the briefing outlines four main users.

26
00:01:52,330 --> 00:02:01,949
First, you have the minister. They use it to brief the cabinet because without it, they really can't accurately describe what the country's digital spend is actually buying.

27
00:02:02,509 --> 00:02:09,140
Right. And then if the minister is one user, I imagine outside players like donors and vendors are the others.

28
00:02:09,210 --> 00:02:10,570
Precisely. Yeah.

29
00:02:10,610 --> 00:02:16,770
A donor uses this picture before funding a new program just to see exactly how their investment fits with existing systems.

30
00:02:16,910 --> 00:02:18,670
To make sure they aren't being redundant, I guess.

31
00:02:18,890 --> 00:02:25,630
Right. And then a vendor uses it to ensure whatever proposed system they wanna build actually matches the environment that is already there.

32
00:02:25,670 --> 00:02:34,630
Which introduces a brilliant dynamic. So we have the minister, the donor, the vendor, but the fourth user is you, the official managing these systems.

33
00:02:34,730 --> 00:02:37,270
Yes, and- Because you hold the blueprint, the EA,

34
00:02:37,910 --> 00:02:40,730
and you use it to keep all three of those other users aligned.

35
00:02:41,010 --> 00:02:45,050
That's a great way to put it. You ensure the donor funds what the country actually needs.

36
00:02:45,390 --> 00:02:50,209
The vendor builds what fits, and the minister can face the cabinet with a coherent story.

37
00:02:50,270 --> 00:02:52,220
So let's, uh, zoom in on this blueprint.

38
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:56,350
The briefing outlines four specific layers to this picture.

39
00:02:56,370 --> 00:02:57,360
What are we looking at first?

40
00:02:57,990 --> 00:03:04,010
First, you map the services. This is simply what your government does and for whom.

41
00:03:04,030 --> 00:03:09,290
It is the layer the minister talks about most because, well, it's what the public actually interacts with.

42
00:03:09,510 --> 00:03:14,910
Right. But if services are what the public sees, there has to be something powering them behind the scenes.

43
00:03:14,950 --> 00:03:16,970
Which brings us to the second layer, data.

44
00:03:17,270 --> 00:03:27,010
Data. Yes. This documents the information your government holds, who owns it, and crucially, where the authoritative copy the register actually

45
00:03:27,170 --> 00:03:27,690
lives.

46
00:03:27,850 --> 00:03:30,280
Oh, and we should make sure we say register, not registered.

47
00:03:30,290 --> 00:03:35,710
That's exactly. Register. And that naturally flows into the third layer, which is applications.

48
00:03:36,270 --> 00:03:40,110
This is the software that actively uses the data to deliver the services.

49
00:03:40,350 --> 00:03:42,540
Wait, I always kinda thought of applications coming first.

50
00:03:42,670 --> 00:03:45,110
Right. But you're saying data comes before applications.

51
00:03:45,250 --> 00:03:48,370
Yes, strictly in that order. Data, then applications.

52
00:03:48,430 --> 00:03:50,690
Because the applications are just there to use the data.

53
00:03:50,850 --> 00:04:00,490
Got it. And then you have the fourth and final layer, technology, the underlying infrastructure like networks, hosting, and security that just has to be running for anything else to function.

54
00:04:00,550 --> 00:04:03,010
Okay, here is a point of confusion for me, though.

55
00:04:03,090 --> 00:04:06,799
Mm. Usually, we think of technology as the most permanent foundation of anything.

56
00:04:07,579 --> 00:04:12,140
But in digital systems, we constantly hear about the rapid pace of tech evolution.

57
00:04:12,869 --> 00:04:15,500
So is technology really the permanent foundation here?

58
00:04:15,810 --> 00:04:19,730
That raises a crucial point, and the answer is emphatically no.

59
00:04:19,870 --> 00:04:22,770
Technology actually cycles roughly every decade.

60
00:04:22,830 --> 00:04:24,510
Wait, really? Just a decade?

61
00:04:24,610 --> 00:04:27,850
Just a decade, and applications come and go even faster than that.

62
00:04:28,190 --> 00:04:32,130
It's actually the data that is the longest-lived part of the entire architecture.

63
00:04:32,610 --> 00:04:34,770
Data outlasts everything else in government.

64
00:04:34,830 --> 00:04:37,500
Wow. Okay, so when a ministry finally sits down

65
00:04:38,190 --> 00:04:42,690
and documents these four-part services, data, applications, technology,

66
00:04:43,690 --> 00:04:46,650
what is the tangible transformation? Like, what's the result?

67
00:04:46,750 --> 00:04:50,430
Your digital landscape stops being a list of unrelated projects.

68
00:04:50,470 --> 00:04:52,910
It becomes a true deliberate system.

69
00:04:52,970 --> 00:04:54,390
You can actually plan around it.

70
00:04:54,490 --> 00:05:04,460
Exactly. You can plan investments against it, and you can finally have substantive discussions across different ministries without having to re-explain the basics every single

71
00:05:04,490 --> 00:05:05,590
time you meet.

72
00:05:05,650 --> 00:05:07,480
Because you finally have that agreed picture.

73
00:05:07,550 --> 00:05:10,710
Precisely. An EA isn't a piece of software you buy.

74
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:17,250
It is the agreed picture that empowers you to dictate what comes next rather than leaving that power to a vendor or donor.

